-- title:
--   The cone gap is $1$-Lipschitz
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ with $\lVert v\rVert = 1$, and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$ satisfy $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. Then for all $p, q \in E$,
--
--   $$\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,p) - \mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,q) \;\le\; \lVert p - q\rVert,$$
--
--   where $\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,p) = \langle v,p\rangle \sin\vartheta - \lVert p - \langle v,p\rangle v\rVert\cos\vartheta$ is the signed distance from $p$ to the boundary of the half-cone with axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta$. Since $p$ and $q$ may be interchanged, this is equivalent to the two-sided statement $|\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,p) - \mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,q)| \le \lVert p-q\rVert$, i.e. the gap is a $1$-Lipschitz function of its point argument.
--
--   Lipschitz continuity with constant exactly $1$ is what makes the gap behave like a distance to the cone boundary: moving a point by $\varepsilon$ costs at most $\varepsilon$ of clearance, so clearance can be budgeted additively along the chains of small displacements that the constructions of Sections 4 and 5 perform.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The statement is given in the one-sided form, which is the form in which it is applied. The hypotheses $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$ are carried because that is the regime in which the cone-gap toolkit is used; they are not what produces the constant $1$. With $\lVert v\rVert = 1$ the map $p \mapsto \bigl(\langle v,p\rangle,\ \lVert p - \langle v,p\rangle v\rVert\bigr) \in \mathbb{R}^2$ is $1$-Lipschitz, and $(a,b) \mapsto a\sin\vartheta - b\cos\vartheta$ is a linear functional of norm $1$ for every real $\vartheta$, so the Lipschitz bound with constant $1$ holds whatever the value of $\vartheta$.
